{
  "dom_id": "step-q335899",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t-2),$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\sqrt{t}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t-2}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t-2}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t}{2\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t}{2\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t}{2\\sqrt{t}} & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061t & >0 \\\\ t & >{e}^{0} \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (1,\\infty ).\n$$",
